WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.820
Bad News Baseball!

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:06.680
Looking for a baseball game that will challenge your skill and tickle your funny bone?

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.660
You've finally found it!

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:31.780
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:33.780
I'm Mike. And I'm Joe.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.040
Still no Sean.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.560
Still no sight of Sean.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:40.780
But we, I don't know, we remain hopeful?

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:42.640
Yeah, I'm talking to him.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:46.040
I'm convincing him we'll get him back on board.

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:47.620
We gotta adjust his contract.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.020
Yeah, or maybe Sam will come back to fill in for Sean.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:53.180
Anything's possible.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.540
We'll bring Sam back.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:58.500
Yeah, maybe just a new person will join the show and just catch up.

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:03.100
We'll just ask them for their Essential Games list vote on the past 300 games.

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:09.000
I mean, if they do that, I've been saying I'm gonna catch up on all the games I've missed for like two years now and I still haven't done it.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.740
So they might replace me.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:15.100
And yet you chose to play Bad News Baseball this week to be on the show.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:16.180
I did. Yeah.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:24.580
I can't be the only one who thought that this was gonna be a game based on the 1976 baseball comedy Bad News Bears, right?

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:34.200
The only reason I thought that was last week when you confused them, and then I suddenly, like, dawned on me, how did I not think of this before?

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.320
Well, there are animals in this game, but there weren't animals in the movie.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.880
And the animals in this game aren't bears.

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.740
They're rabbit umpires.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:56.100
But I think because there's animals in this and there weren't animals in the game, I think it's kind of like the whole Butterfree Venomoth thing where I think the titles actually got swapped by accident.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:01.960
Like, this was actually originally supposed to be Bad News Bears, Bad News Bears, and Bad News Bears was supposed to be Bad News Baseball.

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.760
Titles got swapped and, you know, no one knew.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.700
Right. Okay, yeah, very good.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:16.520
And for those of you who don't know, the same thing is true about Venomoth and Butterfree, that they are part of different evolution lines, but they look exactly like they would evolve into one another.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:26.520
Moving back to video games that we talk about on this show, because this is not a Pokemon show, the back of the box said that it'll challenge your skill and tickle your funny bone.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:33.620
There are a category of silly baseball games like Backyard Baseball and MLB Slugfest.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:46.060
One is funny in the sense of it's a bunch of, like, pro versions, but they're kids now and they're playing Backyard Baseball under, like, those kinds of, you know, rules and silliness.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:54.140
And then the other one's like, oh, this is really silly because you can just beat up the guy running from first to second instead of having to just tag him out.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:03:01.740
Now, I mean, I guess there's, like, there's a rabbit as the ump and there's, like, some silly animations, but, like, not very frequent.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:03.660
Yeah, is this game all that silly?

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.240
No, I wouldn't think so.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:13.080
But, like, maybe the bar hadn't been set for, like, how much silliness saturation there needs to be in order to call your game silly.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.920
Because most games weren't trying to be silly.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.780
So, like, maybe just having a few silly things in there, maybe they thought, oh, this game's so silly.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.620
Yeah, this game is whimsical, I guess is what I would say, right?

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:31.960
Like, you know, there's some cosmetic things like the rabbit umpires and there's some silly cut scenes.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:36.780
Like, if you hit a home run hard enough, it, like, launches out of the atmosphere.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.500
Like, that's cute, right?

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:46.400
But there's nothing like playing baseball in space like there was in Chex Notes Baseball Simulator.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:52.080
So, Baseball Simulator even got the memo of, like, here's how you have fun with a baseball game.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:04:02.160
And yet this one is, like, staying almost entirely true, with the exception of a few rule changes, to baseball and the simulation of baseball on the NES.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.060
And then just being like, but what if the umpires were rabbits?

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:07.700
Like, really, why are the umpires rabbits, Joe?

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:17.140
Yeah, I thought that this was some, like, reference or, like, maybe there was, like, a term that umps were called sometimes that was rabbit-like, but I have no idea.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:27.940
Yeah, and the only thing I can think of is that it must mean something to be, like, humanized as a rabbit in Japan, like the anthropomorphic rabbit thing,

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:38.840
because that must mean something, because this game is based off of the Nippon Professional Baseball League, not, like, MLB rules, per se.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:45.720
And the only reason you can really know that is because the game will end in a tie after 12 innings, just like it does in the Japanese League.

00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:46.860
Oh, okay.

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:50.120
That's the only, you said before some differences.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.460
Is that the only, like, rule difference to, like, the baseball I'd be familiar with?

00:04:53.460 --> 00:05:00.460
It's the only rule difference I caught, and to be honest, I mean, that would imply that it's a pretty faithful kind of game,

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:03.680
because there are other ones that just don't even go to extra innings, right?

00:05:03.760 --> 00:05:05.060
I couldn't think of that off the top of my head,

00:05:05.100 --> 00:05:08.580
but there are certain ones that are just like, if you don't do it in nine, it's over.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.360
Yeah. So, at least they went to the 12.

00:05:11.780 --> 00:05:12.720
Yeah, that's good.

00:05:12.980 --> 00:05:17.960
But there was no seventh inning stretch, and for the silliest game on the NES, or whatever they called it,

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.380
I would think there would be some seventh inning stretch animation.

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:24.740
Oh, well, see, that's a unique thing again, because they don't have a seventh inning stretch over in Japan.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:26.660
Oh, that's American. It wouldn't get added, right?

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:27.780
They didn't add anything.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:35.700
This game was made by Tecmo, who already gave us Tecmo Baseball, literally the name of the developer and baseball,

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:36.940
all in one game.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.580
Why make another one and not just call it Tecmo Baseball 2?

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:47.340
We get, instead, Bad News Baseball, where this game happens to look nothing like Tecmo Baseball?

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:51.900
What's going on? This game looks like RBI Baseball, which was not made by Tecmo.

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:57.900
Well, what I was just saying, I'm actually right now looking up Tecmo Baseball to remind myself what it looks like,

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.560
so I don't embarrass myself here.

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:05.340
But what I'm gathering is that this is kind of geared more towards, like, your little brother.

00:06:06.040 --> 00:06:08.240
You know? Just the art style.

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:15.600
And it's in, you know, playing on, like, normal mode or whatever it is that's not all-star mode is pretty accessible.

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:19.020
Maybe one of the more, I'm not that, like, baseball games are not accessible,

00:06:19.060 --> 00:06:23.600
but, like, maybe one of the easier baseball games to play that we've had in a long time.

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:31.720
So maybe it's all, like, that, the art style combined with that, like, difficulty makes it a little more geared towards a younger audience.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.420
You can see, though, too, yeah, the younger audience thing comes into play when you look at Tecmo Baseball,

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:39.000
now that you've been looking at references of it.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:42.140
Like, that is a, you know, that is much more, like, these are athletes,

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:49.200
this is supposed to be a representation of the MLB, you know, it's a more, like, detailed screen.

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:55.260
Whereas, I don't know, something about the fact that, I mean, and we liked RBI Baseball, too,

00:06:55.260 --> 00:07:02.140
but the fact that they, even the view of the zoomed-in bases and the dirt only, you know,

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:08.680
not connecting the entire diamond, the entire baseball diamond, it's, like, it feels like somehow, you know,

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:13.360
because it's, like, I don't know who made that officially, if it was Tenjin or Namco or whoever,

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:22.220
but it's just weird that this game chose to mirror a completely different developer's style

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:25.180
when they already had, like, something in the can, you know?

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:29.120
Like, they could have just went with Tecmo Baseball but silly,

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.460
and instead they were like, nah, let's just rip off a totally different game.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:40.040
Yeah, I mean, maybe they felt like they needed to differentiate them, like, this game from Tecmo Baseball,

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:42.040
enough to justify it, you know?

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:46.040
Like, why would I buy this other game if I already have this one that you made?

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:49.440
I mean, I don't think consumers really thought so much about the developer back then,

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.420
but maybe they did, I don't know.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:57.080
But, um, so that's the only thing I can think of, where they were, like, trying to,

00:07:57.120 --> 00:07:58.360
how long ago was Tecmo Baseball?

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:00.580
A while ago now.

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:03.240
I can get you a date if you can talk for a second.

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:04.920
Uh, hello, everybody.

00:08:05.120 --> 00:08:06.400
Thank you, that was one second.

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:15.780
And Tecmo Baseball was episode 158, so February 19th, 2021, or January 1st, 1989.

00:08:16.220 --> 00:08:23.040
Okay, so one year later, roughly, a little over a year later, in real time, that this came out, is a little weird.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:27.080
I mean, I guess it's not now, because we get, you know, we get an MLB game every year.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:34.000
But then, it was, it does feel like they're kind of eating their own lunch,

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:37.660
which doesn't have the same meaning as eating someone else's lunch, because you're still eating your lunch.

00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:38.500
But you know what I mean?

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:40.380
Like, they're competing with themselves.

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:46.960
Yeah, and not only are they competing with themselves, but because they didn't go all in on the, like,

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:51.920
this is kiddie, this is super easy, this is super silly.

00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:53.200
Like, they could have chose any of those pads.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:54.320
I'm not saying they had to do all three.

00:08:54.320 --> 00:08:58.280
But because they didn't choose that, and it winds up just playing just like baseball,

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:02.520
it does kind of run into the issue of, like, well, which one would I choose, right?

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:03.860
Like, there's no reason to own both.

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:08.360
You wouldn't buy Tecmo Baseball and Bad News Baseball.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:14.520
And you probably wouldn't buy Bad News Baseball, because Tecmo Baseball is much more straightforward of a product, right?

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:20.200
Like, Bad News Baseball implies that, to me, that there's something more going on here, right?

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:22.320
Like, that's not an everyday name.

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:25.640
It's not like Baseball 2K5, right?

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:26.900
It's not the show.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:31.780
Like, something about Bad News Baseball makes me feel like there was going to be a story mode.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:37.680
Yeah, like, a story mode or just, like, something that is, what's the bad news?

00:09:38.300 --> 00:09:40.020
What is bad news in this game?

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:43.380
Like, that's what's, like, that's what is confusing.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:49.060
The bad news is Tecmo's stock after making two baseball games and competing with each other.

00:09:49.060 --> 00:09:51.900
Like, I hadn't thought about this title until you just mentioned it.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:56.060
But, like, yeah, it's called Bad News Baseball, but, like, maybe that's just a catchy title.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:04.500
But, yeah, I do now think of it, like, yeah, I would think it's, I guess the silliness is what they're going for.

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:05.780
But, I don't know.

00:10:05.940 --> 00:10:11.740
Even with the silliness, I mean, my theory is that they were like, oh, here's a dozen silly animations.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:12.740
This is going to be great.

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:14.360
A dozen? That's a lot of animations.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:18.580
And then not thinking about, like, playing, like, a season of this is, like, 170 hours.

00:10:18.780 --> 00:10:20.900
So, you're spreading out those dozen silly animations.

00:10:21.100 --> 00:10:23.080
Like, it doesn't feel like they come up very often.

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:26.320
Well, the good news is that there isn't really a season.

00:10:26.780 --> 00:10:29.020
This is a baseball game with a goal.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:33.720
And while that sounds interesting, again, the goal is not a story mode.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:35.860
It's not a character development mode.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:42.060
It's just you play as one team, and then your goal is to defeat every other team in the one-player mode.

00:10:42.060 --> 00:10:43.840
So, you only have to beat every team once.

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:48.060
And, in fact, once you beat them within that mode, they are not selectable again.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:54.280
So, it does make it easy to know, like, you know, this is a clearly defined parameter of beat every team.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:04.200
So, you know, that's kind of cool in one way, but then also not too different from a season when they could have made it something different.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:11.720
We have played other sports games on our Nostalgia Bites episodes, which were for Famicom-exclusive games.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:17.740
We've played some of those sports games that have little, like, story beats or cut scenes in between.

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:22.500
And it feels like that's what this game needed is, like, you kind of have the generic team,

00:11:22.580 --> 00:11:26.860
and then you take on all these teams that have, like, oh, this one has really good pitchers.

00:11:26.860 --> 00:11:28.640
This one has really good batters, you know?

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:32.860
Like, and you work around that stuff and try to make sure you defeat them all.

00:11:33.200 --> 00:11:37.680
Instead, this just winds up being, like, you're just playing a really shortened season where you play everybody once.

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:43.720
Yeah. I mean, it's, I guess that also, again, it's little brother mode, you know?

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:50.760
It's, like, maybe a little kid doesn't care enough to, like, figure out the nuances of an actual season of baseball.

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:55.540
And, I mean, there are stats and stuff in this, too, so maybe that kind of hurts that theory.

00:11:55.720 --> 00:11:57.660
But, yeah, it's, like, a season.

00:11:57.860 --> 00:11:58.720
Beat everybody else.

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:02.900
You're done. Joe, they did take the time to put in stats for everybody, as you mentioned,

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:07.020
and yet they don't give you the option to just go full season, right?

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:12.440
Like, it's a little strange that even just things, like, surprising.

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:20.480
So, since there's not a set schedule of games you can play anybody, you would think that your pitchers, you know, can be played whenever.

00:12:20.480 --> 00:12:30.900
But they actually do have a stamina rating that, you know, if you use them too often, it will take, you have to play a few games for them to recover.

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:39.080
But it's not something that's, like, communicated in the same way that a season, like, it feels like these are all exhibition matches, not the season.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:47.660
Because there isn't an overall standings or a trade players or a, you know, a stats page that evolves with the game.

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:50.100
Like, it's just the stats that are built into the characters.

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:56.680
I feel like the game wanted to, like, be two different things, right?

00:12:56.680 --> 00:13:03.260
It wanted to be, like, oh, baseball but with a clearly defined goal, but also baseball that's silly.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:04.560
And it didn't really do either.

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:08.620
Yeah, I mean, it's hard for me to, like, to really say.

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:15.560
Because whenever I play a baseball game or really any sports game, like, I play it, I play two games, and then I'm like, okay, I get it.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:22.340
Like, I'm not a good judge of, like, the season mode or the whatever, the goal mode.

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:25.660
Because it just doesn't stick with me.

00:13:25.660 --> 00:13:29.380
Like, I don't, the desire to keep playing doesn't stick with me enough.

00:13:33.460 --> 00:13:42.360
There is an all-star mode in this game, which I guess if you really dig into this game, if you, if this was your only copy of a baseball game,

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:48.140
and you really spend a lot of time with it and learn about the best players, this mode could be worthwhile.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:58.180
But it's kind of silly when they're all just made-up characters to be like, I'm going to build a team of just the best ones and pit them against another team.

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:09.480
It feels like, I don't know, it feels like maybe if you could have built the characters and stats from the ground up like we saw in other baseball games, that would have been something cool.

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:14.740
But just to have, like, you know, here's, you know, here's Joe.

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:17.420
And it's just Joe, right?

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:21.320
It's not like you're not playing as any specific Joe.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:25.620
It's just the Joe that was introduced in this game that you've never grown up adoring.

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:28.140
It's not the Joe from Nostalgia.

00:14:28.500 --> 00:14:30.480
Yeah. Joe Jackson.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:37.520
The game also features the ability to play as female characters, female baseball players.

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:39.660
Did you know this? I didn't know this.

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:44.020
How do you? They don't want you to know this, but it's weird that it's all in there.

00:14:44.140 --> 00:14:54.000
Okay. So in girls mode, as they call it, the teams remain the same, but the rosters are completely different, including stats and stuff.

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:56.880
So it's not like they just change, you know, they're changing sprites.

00:14:57.040 --> 00:14:58.680
They're changing names.

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:05.200
It's basically 12 new teams if you can get past the fact that the uniforms remain the same.

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:13.260
And the only way to do this is by holding down and left on controller one and holding up on controller two.

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:18.020
Now, keep in mind, you have to also still be holding down and left on controller one at the same time.

00:15:18.640 --> 00:15:23.640
Provided you're doing that, you hit the reset button on your NES, and then it boots up with the girls.

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:29.000
You need to introduce like a toe into the equation to be holding both of those and hit the reset.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:30.540
You can't play it unless you have a friend.

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:45.120
Yeah. Well, so that's just mind-blowing to me because, first of all, they've put, like you said, it's not just like a sprite swap, but actual stat swaps.

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:51.880
And then they put all this work into making, I'm assuming, newly balanced teams.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:16:08.800
And, like, first of all, I don't think anyone, if you're not trading or doing anything with those sets, and this game is kind of, seems to be geared toward a younger audience, I don't know how many people are really, like, doing the math on which team is better based on those stats.

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:09.700
So, already.

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:18.600
But then you're taking this additional double work that you've done and hiding it behind two controllers and a button on the NES.

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:25.780
So, it's like, it's just like almost like extra pointless work that they did because they hid it all.

00:16:25.860 --> 00:16:33.280
And they focused on things that they didn't, like, then elevate in their, I don't know, marketing?

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.320
Or not even marketing, but in their, like, just in the game.

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:42.060
Like, it just doesn't seem like it encourages you to really, to get any appreciation out of that.

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:44.900
Right. And here comes the crazy thing, Joe.

00:16:45.020 --> 00:16:50.240
When I told you how it's performed, right, you would think, what, cheat code, right?

00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:52.820
Like, a specific set of inputs.

00:16:53.040 --> 00:16:54.480
This is in the manual.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:59.820
This is like, hey, if you want to access the girls mode, just do this super complicated thing.

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:06.760
And so, it's not even like, you know, this feels like the kind of thing that you would sell in a book of, like, 101 Nintendo Entertainment System secrets.

00:17:07.340 --> 00:17:08.340
Yeah. It's not.

00:17:08.460 --> 00:17:09.300
It's in the manual.

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:10.500
They tell you how to do it.

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:18.400
And even have, like, a little asterisk for, unfortunately, in this mode, the zoom-up display shows only boy players.

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.360
Sorry for letting you down.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:24.780
Wow. It's like, they're apologizing for, like, rushing the game out the door.

00:17:24.780 --> 00:17:28.160
Like, we could have put them in, but we just didn't have the time.

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:32.080
That is, that is, this is just getting weirder and weirder with everything.

00:17:34.200 --> 00:17:40.000
But it makes you feel like they didn't need to do all those steps, and it could have just been a selectable, like, thing on the main menu, right?

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:41.520
You could just select girls mode.

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:42.800
Yeah. Oh, yeah.

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:54.700
Completely. But also, like, any other company would have just, it would have just been one of those things where it's like, the, yeah, it's weird that the, that the sprites don't, the zoom-up sprites don't change.

00:17:54.700 --> 00:18:05.500
That's annoying. But, like, they called it out in, like, this, this, like, this, like, self-deprecating or, like, self-ashamed way of, like, we're sorry we let you down.

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:06.580
We know we did wrong.

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:14.120
My fourth grade teacher did the equivalent of the girls mode manual thing to us on a test once.

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:26.060
And so the test was, you take the whole test, and if you just would have looked where you put your name in, above that, it said, if you are reading all the instructions, like, do not fill out the test.

00:18:26.260 --> 00:18:28.200
And obviously, like, I missed that.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:29.720
I filled out the test, and I failed.

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:37.480
That's a great test. It's the same thing here, where they're like, let's put the girls mode at the end of the manual just to make sure everybody read through the manual.

00:18:37.660 --> 00:18:40.140
So I actually just looked up the developers of TechMone.

00:18:40.260 --> 00:18:44.240
One of them is Mike's fourth grade teacher was one of the developers.

00:18:44.680 --> 00:18:46.160
They still go by that name, huh?

00:18:46.700 --> 00:18:52.380
Yeah. It's my, like, M-A-I, fourth grade teacher, one word, last name.

00:18:59.760 --> 00:19:07.060
There was one significant improvement, though, in the simulation of baseball in this game.

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:08.780
And, Joe, let me know if you agree.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:12.240
I have an explanation for why I think it's better, too.

00:19:12.380 --> 00:19:20.740
But really, the fielding is significantly improved from past NES games for Bad News Baseball.

00:19:20.740 --> 00:19:26.960
I really felt like I knew who I was going to be on the screen when the ball got hit.

00:19:27.080 --> 00:19:35.060
And I did feel like I was able to field almost all kinds of hits, whether they were high pop-ups or line drives.

00:19:35.240 --> 00:19:36.260
What do you think?

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:39.120
I mostly agree.

00:19:39.340 --> 00:19:44.840
I'm curious what your reasoning is, because I didn't really think about it until now.

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:48.260
But, yeah, like, it was, I did not have those same frustrations.

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:49.480
And I don't really know why.

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:56.280
But I will say that I, the, my agreement is limited to the infield.

00:19:56.860 --> 00:20:02.820
Because in the outfield, I think, I don't remember if other baseball games were this frustrating to me.

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:09.900
But I think that, I think that something needs to change in video game baseball when it comes to the outfield.

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:14.820
The outfield is the issue, though, specifically in this version and in many other versions.

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:19.720
For you, I imagine, because you can't see them until a limited amount of time.

00:20:19.760 --> 00:20:33.120
I have no idea where they are until it's actually too late to even know, to even know, like, even once I can see my outfielder and see where they are and, like, orient myself, I actually don't have enough time to get to the ball.

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:35.460
Where I would have if I knew where my fielder was.

00:20:36.000 --> 00:20:49.180
And 3D baseball games solve this by saying, like, well, when the balls hit and you're playing defense, we will cut to the fielder of choice that you'll be controlling and see, you know, and see it kind of from, like, over the shoulder.

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:51.700
Not really over the shoulder. From behind the full body.

00:20:52.400 --> 00:20:54.220
2D games never really did that, though, right?

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:59.740
They don't, like, a great baseball game on the NES might just, like, zoom in on the shortstop, right?

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:03.180
And make the whole field bigger and you know, like, okay, I have to get the ball.

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:05.720
Like, I'm this character and only this character.

00:21:06.260 --> 00:21:07.560
Yeah. I mean, and I like that.

00:21:07.600 --> 00:21:15.100
I don't think that that's a perfect solution because it is, it does feel like it's, like, it's almost playing a little bit for you by, like, I mean, it's hard to say.

00:21:15.100 --> 00:21:21.060
Because, like, in a baseball video game, I am, like, yeah, I need to figure out where this ball is going and whose ball it is.

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:25.560
But, like, if you're playing baseball and you're the shortstop, yeah, that's all.

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:29.440
You know you're the shortstop and you know that, like, you need to get the ball.

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:34.000
Like, and it's the same thing with, like, the fielders.

00:21:34.000 --> 00:21:40.700
Like, I would like something that would simulate me knowing where I am as the right fielder.

00:21:40.880 --> 00:21:46.940
Like, even if it's just an arrow pointing off screen to, like, roughly where my right fielder is or whatever.

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:55.280
Like, it feels unrealistically unfair to just be blind until your outfielder comes into view.

00:21:55.280 --> 00:22:06.220
And the reason why I think the fielding is better here is because there are rules that feel obvious about who you'll control.

00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:16.640
And this could be a chicken or the egg kind of problem where, like, I read these rules on GameFAQ's, like, strategy guide thing before playing the game.

00:22:16.640 --> 00:22:17.980
And so I was aware of them.

00:22:17.980 --> 00:22:28.360
But I don't recall in other games it being this intuitive about, like, well, depending on where the ball is heading, here's who you will be controlling.

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:32.740
And so there are kind of rules about, like, you know, it defaults to the pitcher.

00:22:33.120 --> 00:22:39.200
But if the ball's hit foul but not bunted, you'll be the catcher.

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:43.780
If the ball's hit somewhere on the right field line, you become the first baseman.

00:22:43.780 --> 00:22:46.340
Well, the second baseman will automatically cover first.

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:47.900
Like, there's all these different rules.

00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:50.540
And I can't, you know, it's such a long thing to describe.

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:51.720
I'm not going to get into it now.

00:22:52.320 --> 00:23:00.640
But once you know kind of, oh, well, it is based on where the ball is hit, you can get your brain kind of in tune with that.

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:03.260
And maybe it's because I know the rules of baseball pretty well.

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:12.320
You can get your brain in tune with that and be able to make less of the mistakes that real baseball players wouldn't make,

00:23:12.420 --> 00:23:15.380
which is what you're always trying to. You're trying to have, like, a real game of baseball here.

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:20.300
You don't want the novice level, like, oh, the ground ball got through the legs, right?

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:23.080
It's like, no, because it doesn't get through the legs when you miss it in these games.

00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:24.420
You'll catch anything you scoop up.

00:23:24.540 --> 00:23:28.560
It's that the ground ball got past you because you were running in the wrong direction.

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:32.400
Tell me when that's ever happened in a game of Major League Baseball, right?

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:36.520
Nobody runs, like, you know, the ball was hit towards second and he went right, you know?

00:23:36.620 --> 00:23:40.200
Yeah, it's sort of like three other players on the field, like, at the exact same time.

00:23:40.200 --> 00:23:41.340
Yes, exactly.

00:23:41.620 --> 00:23:46.960
So I think having these defined rules, which I'm sure every baseball game actually has,

00:23:47.180 --> 00:23:53.240
but for them to be clear enough to be just a paragraph in a game FAQ's strategy guide,

00:23:53.340 --> 00:24:00.180
I think says a lot about the thought that went into what is usually a problem in these NES baseball games.

00:24:00.180 --> 00:24:10.320
Yeah, and I think that going back to your chicken or the egg question, I think that it's – I don't think it has – it's because you read the FAQ first.

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:15.040
Because I didn't know anything about these rules until right now, and it did feel more intuitive to me.

00:24:15.220 --> 00:24:23.080
I mean, I think it's just because, like, yeah, you – I mean, maybe from knowing the rules of baseball or maybe just from seeing where the ball is going,

00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:30.860
when the ball is hit, I just intuitively am like, okay, well, you know, I want to control my shortstop here because – you know, or whatever.

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:39.020
Like, whoever it is, like, of course if the ball is hit, like, to third base, I'm not going to be trying to, like, control my first baseman, you know?

00:24:39.180 --> 00:24:48.480
Like, so there is, like, a level of intuition there that it seems like they factored in very well to the point where I don't even see it.

00:24:48.580 --> 00:24:50.700
It just works in the infield.

00:24:50.700 --> 00:24:52.440
Yeah, that's well said.

00:24:52.940 --> 00:25:04.680
And while we were talking about the silliness of the cutscenes, I do think that another thing that enhances the gameplay are the cutscenes of when you have a close play at the bases.

00:25:05.200 --> 00:25:15.980
And it goes to a, you know, like a cinematic style out versus safe where you see the players slide into the bag and see the umpire make the decision.

00:25:15.980 --> 00:25:23.480
That is exciting because they cut away before it is obvious what the result is on camera.

00:25:23.480 --> 00:25:27.880
So you really don't know going into the cutscene if you're safe or out.

00:25:27.880 --> 00:25:28.940
Yeah, yeah.

00:25:30.200 --> 00:25:37.780
Granted, I was also pleased because there were some times where, like, beforehand I was like, I'm clearly about to be safe.

00:25:37.860 --> 00:25:45.200
Then it goes to the cutscene, and I was always worried that it's just, like, the cutscene is, like, randomly rolling whether I'm safe or out or something.

00:25:45.440 --> 00:25:46.260
But it always felt bad.

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:47.980
That actually is true.

00:25:48.220 --> 00:25:54.140
And I think the reason I had the same thought is because there was one where I was obviously safe.

00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:55.200
And I was ruled safe.

00:25:55.200 --> 00:25:59.160
But there's that moment when you see the cutscene where you're like, is this up to Chance?

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:00.500
Yeah, yeah. Yeah.

00:26:01.120 --> 00:26:02.020
I agree with you.

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.880
And then, so what do you think of, should they have done more of that?

00:26:04.880 --> 00:26:07.300
Or does that, like, slow down the game too much?

00:26:07.300 --> 00:26:19.280
Should they have made it where, like, if you're obviously hovering over a pop-up, that it should switch to a cutscene showing you, like, catching that ball in a more awesome way?

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:23.100
Or should they, or is that, like, now that breaks up the action too much?

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:30.860
I'd say it happens enough to make those moments feel, like, elevated, exciting moments, but not so much that I get sick of it.

00:26:30.880 --> 00:26:33.820
And I'm like, okay, like, I don't want to see cutscene after cutscene.

00:26:34.280 --> 00:26:41.120
Not that they're long, but, yeah, I think that, like, having them spread out more makes them more of a novelty.

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:56.840
And I feel like any other problems I would have with, you know, this version of baseball, because it's just so similar to the kind of RBI baseball and similar games we played, it's like, yeah, those are just the, they haven't been fixed, but they're not necessarily worse either.

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:02.360
So I don't know, like, where this ranks in the baseball games that we played.

00:27:02.360 --> 00:27:03.800
We'll get into that in a minute.

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:11.980
But overall, you know, it's kind of just another, like, another entry here.

00:27:12.160 --> 00:27:14.440
It didn't twist baseball enough.

00:27:14.440 --> 00:27:21.720
I think the back of the box sold me on silly baseball game, and it didn't deliver on that front.

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:28.980
Yeah, I mean, if we are going to get into where it ranks, like, I'll tell you right now, like, I don't know.

00:27:29.460 --> 00:27:32.060
Like, all these baseball, they're all baseball.

00:27:32.320 --> 00:27:37.460
Like, there are some that are different, but, like, they are starting to blur together to me, where I'm like, I thought this one was fun.

00:27:37.460 --> 00:27:39.360
I think I thought a bunch of other ones were fun, too.

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:41.740
So, like, that's a tough one.

00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:45.000
Well, I have good news about bad news baseball.

00:27:45.280 --> 00:27:50.060
And that is that it's time for its judgment on the Essential Games List.

00:27:53.320 --> 00:27:54.760
Should have said it was up to bat.

00:27:55.720 --> 00:27:57.300
Hmm. Yeah, whatever.

00:27:57.580 --> 00:28:01.300
Maybe next week in whatever baseball game we're playing next week.

00:28:01.880 --> 00:28:03.060
Yeah. Well, you know what?

00:28:03.260 --> 00:28:05.040
There's three strikes and you're out, right?

00:28:05.700 --> 00:28:10.400
But really, it just takes one strike for a baseball game to get off the Essential Games List.

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:16.380
And I'm not saying that this is a better or worse game than what we've got.

00:28:16.580 --> 00:28:19.320
It's just clearly more of the same.

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:31.640
So, like we were just getting at a moment before, until a baseball game does something that, like, radically changes what a baseball game is like on the NES, these are going to continue to remain off.

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:33.100
So, for me, it's a no.

00:28:33.540 --> 00:28:36.820
Joe? Yeah, I mean, almost exactly the same sentiment.

00:28:37.060 --> 00:28:38.020
I think this is fine.

00:28:38.860 --> 00:28:42.080
This is just as fine as a bunch of other baseball games we've played.

00:28:42.240 --> 00:28:46.640
We mentioned some things that have done better with, like, the fielding and stuff, but not.

00:28:47.360 --> 00:28:50.200
It still just feels like most of those other games.

00:28:50.740 --> 00:28:54.060
So, yeah, it's fine, but it's not Essential.

00:28:54.880 --> 00:29:00.880
One thing we didn't talk about now that it's off the Essential Games List, and this is neither a pro or con against the game.

00:29:00.880 --> 00:29:06.920
Are you aware of the, like, little cheat to just always be safe?

00:29:07.240 --> 00:29:12.240
No. Against these, yeah, there's a, it's not always, it's like, you know, it does require some skill on the player.

00:29:12.240 --> 00:29:17.480
But essentially, if you are safe, right?

00:29:17.620 --> 00:29:19.280
Like, let's say you're safe at second, right?

00:29:21.340 --> 00:29:24.280
Just head to, no, sorry, you're not safe at second.

00:29:24.280 --> 00:29:35.820
If you're on your way to second, just head to third, and you can advance the player behind you while just heading right back because they'll throw the ball to the empty bag.

00:29:35.820 --> 00:29:40.500
So, you can just kind of always keep your players going in, like, a constant conga line.

00:29:40.500 --> 00:29:42.960
As long as you have one bag open, you know?

00:29:43.340 --> 00:29:54.180
Like, you, if you, if you twitch, I didn't explain that perfectly, but basically, if you just twitch a player in a certain direction, the computers will automatically throw the ball in the direction you were heading.

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:55.400
Yeah.

00:30:24.180 --> 00:30:25.960
Hit both heading at the same time.

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:32.980
They're going to throw to home to stop the run, but you just return back to third, and the guy from first gets to second safely.

00:30:32.980 --> 00:30:34.780
I noticed a similar thing happen.

00:30:34.940 --> 00:30:39.660
Now, this only happened to me once where I basically got into a pickle, but there was just no pickle.

00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:47.520
Like, you know, like, there was a pop-up or something, or I tried to, I don't remember, I tried to take an extra base and run to second, whatever it was.

00:30:47.520 --> 00:30:55.760
And then, I'm, like, almost at second base when I, like, realize, like, when I remember how to turn back or figure out how to turn back.

00:30:56.040 --> 00:31:03.340
I turn back, and, like, from basically, like, a foot away from second base, I run all the way back to first base, and the second baseman just holds the ball.

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:05.300
Does not throw it back to first base.

00:31:05.440 --> 00:31:07.600
Like, they do not put me in, like, a pickle.

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:09.700
Guys never heard of monkey in the middle.

00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:11.240
Yeah, I don't know why.

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:12.660
I was just like, why was that free?

00:31:13.020 --> 00:31:15.700
But it was, and I wonder if it always would happen that way.

00:31:15.700 --> 00:31:22.460
Next week, we'll be playing a game called Boulder Dash, but I don't know, that sounds like nonsense to me.

00:31:22.920 --> 00:31:29.360
I think that is the fourth or the eighth level of Crash Bandicoot 1.

00:31:29.640 --> 00:31:33.860
Great. Oh, you know, Joe, there was something I wanted to talk to you about, so we'll do that in the post-show.

00:32:00.500 --> 00:32:02.400
Crash Bandicoot, since you mentioned it.

00:32:02.800 --> 00:32:06.520
I found out, because you also play those Uncharted games, right?

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:10.580
Mm-hmm. So, in Uncharted 4, there was a Crash Bandicoot?

00:32:10.780 --> 00:32:12.800
Like, you play the exact level?

00:32:12.800 --> 00:32:14.520
How did it work? You play...

00:32:15.020 --> 00:32:16.820
I don't know why I'm bringing this up on the show.

00:32:16.960 --> 00:32:18.200
This has nothing to do with anything.

00:32:18.500 --> 00:32:31.200
It's funny, because Boulder Dash, which is, I just looked it up, it's the 15th level, I was way off, but Boulder Dash is, like, the sequel level to an earlier level, which is level 4, which is called Boulders.

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:45.620
Boulders is the level you play in Uncharted 4, where you sit down on the couch as Nathan Drake and put on your PlayStation 1, and you play through that level of Crash Bandicoot.

00:32:45.920 --> 00:32:47.240
Okay, now hold on, a couple questions.

00:32:47.540 --> 00:32:49.040
So, Uncharted 4 is a prequel?

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:53.980
No. Okay, so all the other games take place in the past?

00:32:54.940 --> 00:32:56.380
Why does he have a PS1?

00:32:57.120 --> 00:33:00.960
Oh, I don't know, she just has a PS1, his wife just has a PS1, I don't know.

00:33:00.960 --> 00:33:04.180
Got it, oh yeah, they just leave all their PlayStation consoles connected.

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:09.640
They're the retro gamers. He's been out climbing mountains and jumping off of architecture and shit.

00:33:09.640 --> 00:33:12.940
Yeah, and now he's playing every PlayStation 1 game in chronological order.

00:33:13.020 --> 00:33:13.920
No, he's catching...

00:33:13.920 --> 00:33:15.040
yeah, exactly, he's got a podcast.

00:33:15.780 --> 00:33:18.100
But one thing that's cool, did you play Uncharted 4?

00:33:18.280 --> 00:33:21.120
I haven't played any Uncharted, that's why I'm trying to figure out what's going on here.

00:33:21.260 --> 00:33:22.200
Great, great games.

00:33:22.920 --> 00:33:29.300
But I don't know if you will permit me a minor, non-story-related gameplay spoiler for Uncharted 4.

00:33:29.300 --> 00:33:33.280
I'll allow it, but I'm glad you said it for the listeners at home.

00:33:33.340 --> 00:33:39.600
Yes. If you don't want this minor spoiler, finish the episode.

00:33:41.140 --> 00:33:47.180
But at the very end of Uncharted 4, I never see anyone talking about this, but I'm pretty

00:33:47.180 --> 00:33:48.440
sure this was intentional.

00:33:48.680 --> 00:33:51.940
It's not like the very, very end, but it's close to before the final boss.

00:33:51.940 --> 00:33:58.500
There is a run-and-gun kind of section that they've done before, but you're running from

00:33:58.500 --> 00:33:59.840
a giant truck.

00:34:00.840 --> 00:34:04.740
The camera is in front of you, and you're running away from it, and you're shooting back at it.

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:07.600
And that is this level of Crash Bandicoot, you're running from a boulder.

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:11.820
And I think that that was thematically meant to be a little nod.

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:13.060
Which is also like a...

00:34:13.060 --> 00:34:16.440
I thought that was really cool. Isn't that just a thematic nod to Indiana Jones?

00:34:16.840 --> 00:34:18.700
Yeah. Which is what also Uncharted is?

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:20.560
Yeah, that's a good point, too.

00:34:20.720 --> 00:34:25.960
But it's not... The thing is, I would think it'd be more a thematic nod to Indiana Jones if Nathan Drake

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:27.480
was running from a round boulder.

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:32.560
But I guess you're right. I mean, the level in Crash Bandicoot is a nod to Indiana Jones.

00:34:32.760 --> 00:34:33.940
So, wow.

00:34:34.100 --> 00:34:35.600
There's a lot of connections here.

00:34:36.000 --> 00:34:38.280
Also, just have to unpack this.

00:34:38.560 --> 00:34:45.120
You saying Uncharted 4, the end, and right before the final boss...

00:34:45.120 --> 00:34:47.380
The statement final boss sounds crazy to me.

00:34:47.460 --> 00:34:48.680
And I don't play these games.

00:34:48.840 --> 00:34:52.500
But that's like hearing like, oh, you know the final boss of Grand Theft Auto V?

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:55.680
It's like we just don't call them that kind of thing.

00:34:55.720 --> 00:34:56.720
But it is. It is.

00:34:56.820 --> 00:34:59.000
And the thing is that there's been what...

00:34:59.000 --> 00:35:03.960
I think Uncharted 3 didn't really have a boss in the same way that the other ones did.

00:35:03.960 --> 00:35:10.680
But they've tried to make the Uncharted formula work with a boss in each of their games.

00:35:11.320 --> 00:35:13.260
And it's always been really weird.

00:35:13.300 --> 00:35:15.220
And it still is weird in Uncharted 4.

00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:19.160
But I think that they perfected it in Uncharted 4.

00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:20.540
It's not a difficult thing.

00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:22.340
It's very cinematic.

00:35:22.420 --> 00:35:26.240
But it feels like a very good culmination of the gameplay out.

00:35:26.360 --> 00:35:29.760
It feels like, oh yeah, this is how you do a boss in an Uncharted game.

00:35:29.760 --> 00:35:32.700
And so I guess I'm just going to have to start playing these Uncharted games.

00:35:32.940 --> 00:35:37.320
But true or false, Uncharted 1 didn't age well.

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:41.140
Yeah, true. I mean, I still enjoy it.

00:35:41.200 --> 00:35:42.560
The aiming is really weird.

00:35:43.260 --> 00:35:45.520
I think that that's probably like the biggest thing.

00:35:46.000 --> 00:35:50.060
Isn't there like a big thing plot-wise though that's also like didn't age well?

00:35:50.800 --> 00:35:51.680
I don't...

00:35:51.680 --> 00:35:54.700
I'd have to watch a recap of the plot.

00:35:54.860 --> 00:35:55.780
It has been a long time.

00:35:55.880 --> 00:35:57.440
But I don't remember anything that was like...

00:35:57.440 --> 00:35:59.560
I don't remember anything that was like cancelable.

00:35:59.560 --> 00:36:01.300
Okay. No, no, not cancelable.

00:36:01.860 --> 00:36:03.600
Spoilers, I guess, for Uncharted 1.

00:36:03.600 --> 00:36:05.080
Or maybe this is Uncharted 2.

00:36:05.340 --> 00:36:07.260
I'm pretty sure it's 1 though because 2 is beloved.

00:36:08.140 --> 00:36:10.040
Don't like zombies show up at a point?

00:36:10.120 --> 00:36:12.020
Oh, I wish you didn't know that.

00:36:12.100 --> 00:36:15.520
But yes. But I think that that's not it aging poorly.

00:36:15.700 --> 00:36:17.900
When I first played that, I was like, what the hell?

00:36:17.980 --> 00:36:19.560
This is such a realistic game.

00:36:19.640 --> 00:36:20.500
And now they're just throwing zombies.

00:36:20.960 --> 00:36:22.160
But now I look back on it.

00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:23.560
It was an awesome twist.

00:36:24.040 --> 00:36:25.400
You had no idea it was coming.

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:26.840
And the whole game changes.

00:36:26.840 --> 00:36:28.880
It becomes not like a cover shooter.

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:31.500
Suddenly it is like a run and gun, like run for your life.

00:36:31.800 --> 00:36:33.760
Because you don't expect there to be zombies.

00:36:33.780 --> 00:36:36.060
You don't expect it to be scary or to be like...

00:36:36.580 --> 00:36:37.960
And all of a sudden that's what it is.

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:42.800
And a lot of the Uncharted games have supernatural elements.

00:36:42.840 --> 00:36:43.980
It's like Indiana Jones. You know?

00:36:44.100 --> 00:36:48.840
There's like... It's almost like when I think back, like why didn't I expect there to be some supernatural thing?

00:36:48.940 --> 00:36:49.880
That's what it's based on.

00:36:50.060 --> 00:36:52.080
Indiana Jones always has some supernatural thing.

00:36:52.900 --> 00:36:55.820
Your reaction, I wish you hadn't heard that.

00:36:56.080 --> 00:36:59.620
I can't think of too many other video games where I would have that kind of reaction.

00:36:59.840 --> 00:37:01.640
Like, oh, I wish you didn't know that.

00:37:01.740 --> 00:37:02.580
Yeah, that wasn't spoiled.

00:37:02.580 --> 00:37:05.540
It's funny. It doesn't happen that often anymore.

00:37:06.100 --> 00:37:08.700
Yeah. I get it with books and movies.

00:37:08.900 --> 00:37:11.020
I mean, you know, Harry Potter, there's still stuff that like...

00:37:11.020 --> 00:37:12.520
It's impossible to not know.

00:37:12.640 --> 00:37:14.680
But I wish people didn't know before they read the books.

00:37:14.980 --> 00:37:19.380
You know, there was a moment in God of War 2018 that I'm not going to discuss here.

00:37:19.380 --> 00:37:21.480
Because I wish you hadn't heard it.

00:37:22.580 --> 00:37:28.280
But like that, there is a moment in God of War 2018 where it's like, it's cool if you don't know that's coming.

00:37:28.480 --> 00:37:30.100
So I'll leave it at that. Yeah.
